window.docContents[50].resolve({"/Connectivity/#section-trees-and-bridges":{"contents":"A graph is acyclic if and only if every edge is a bridge. For example, every edge of\npathNat₁ is a bridge.\n\n#check isAcyclic_iff_forall_adj_isBridge\n\n\nexample {n : ℕ} : pathNat₁.IsBridge s(n, n + 1) :=\n  isAcyclic_iff_forall_adj_isBridge.mp pathNat₁_isAcyclic (by simp)\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Connectivity\u0009Trees","header":"10.2.4. Trees and Bridges","id":"/Connectivity/#section-trees-and-bridges"},"/Distance/#section-diameter-of-box-products":{"contents":"Since distances add in a box product, so do diameters, provided both factors have a\nvertex (if one factor has no vertices then neither does the product, and its diameter\nis 0). The upper bound is immediate from edist_boxProd. For the lower bound we\nwrite each diameter as a single supremum over pairs of vertices (ediam_def) and use\nENat.iSup_add_iSup_le, which bounds a sum of two suprema term by term.\n\nlemma ediam_boxProd {α β : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph α} {H : SimpleGraph β}\n    [Nonempty α] [Nonempty β] : (G □ H).ediam = G.ediam + H.ediam := by\n  refine le_antisymm (ediam_le_of_edist_le fun x y => ?_) ?_\n  · rw [edist_boxProd]\n    exact add_le_add edist_le_ediam edist_le_ediam\n  · rw [ediam_def, ediam_def]\n    refine ENat.iSup_add_iSup_le fun p q => ?_\n    simpa using edist_le_ediam (G := G □ H) (u := (p.1, q.1)) (v := (p.2, q.2))\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance\u0009Eccentricity, Diameter and Radius","header":"11.2.2. Diameter of Box Products","id":"/Distance/#section-diameter-of-box-products"},"/Walks/#section-walks-in-hypercubes":{"contents":"We will consider the two most natural walks in the hypercube: canonical shortest\npaths and Gray code paths.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks\u0009Walks in Hypercube and Mycielskian Graphs","header":"8.7.1. Walks in Hypercubes","id":"/Walks/#section-walks-in-hypercubes"}});